page("how-to-use-a-compact-charcoal-grill","How to Use a Compact Charcoal Grill (Start to Finish)","Unbox, assemble, season, light, build heat zones, cook and pack down the Slot Grill Compact: the complete step-by-step routine for first-timers and regulars.",IMG["c28"],f"""
<p>A compact charcoal grill rewards a little planning. You have less room than on a big grill, so where you put the coals matters more, and the food sits close to the fire, so things move fast. This is the whole routine on the Slot Grill Compact, from flat pack to cleanup, written so a first-timer can get it right the first time.</p><blockquote>"I am completely new to charcoal grilling, but this little grill works great! … And this thing is beefier than I expected, I expect this grill to easily last the rest of my life."<cite>George Ochoa, Compact owner</cite></blockquote>
<h2>Know the grill</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Cooking area</th><td>16.25" × 9" (146 sq in), sized for one to three people</td></tr><tr><th>Footprint / height</th><td>17.5" × 10.25" · 10.5" tall</td></tr><tr><th>Steel</th><td>12-gauge carbon steel</td></tr><tr><th>Weight</th><td>About 14 lb (16.2 lb with 1 griddle, 18.4 lb with 2)</td></tr><tr><th>Air gap</th><td>2⅞" between the table and the bottom of the coal bed</td></tr><tr><th>Underside</th><td>The Spine, a formed steel bottom: a smooth underside with no loose pieces to pinch your hand</td></tr><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Tab and slot, no tools, no hinges, 30–60 seconds</td></tr><tr><th>Packed</th><td>About 1" thick</td></tr><tr><th>Fuel</th><td>Charcoal or cooking wood</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>1. Before the first cook: season it</h2><p>The Compact is raw carbon steel. Wash it, dry it, and season it with a thin coat of oil or <a href="{BALM}">Slot Grill Seasoning Balm</a> baked at 400°F for 1 hour. Full steps: <a href="/how-to-season-a-compact-grill.html">how to season</a>.</p>
<h2>2. Pick a safe spot</h2><p>Set up on a stable, level, non-combustible surface: bare soil, gravel, sand, concrete, or a fire-safe pad. Keep clear of tents, vehicles, siding, dry grass and anything that can burn. Never use a charcoal grill indoors, in a garage, tent or vehicle. Burning charcoal makes carbon monoxide.</p>
<h2>3. Assemble it (about 30–60 seconds)</h2><ol><li>Hold one end piece with the <b>SLOT GRILL</b> text facing out.</li><li>Slide the two tabs of the two-tab side panel into its slots.</li><li>Add the second end piece, text facing out. The end pieces are universal.</li><li>Set the remaining side panel's top tabs onto the end pieces and slide its three bottom tabs into the first panel. The sides lock into a tight V.</li><li>Check every tab is fully seated and the grill sits level. Wiggle it: it should feel solid.</li></ol><p>If a tab is stiff, check for grit or seasoning buildup in the slot and wipe it clean. Don't force it with a hammer.</p>
<h2>4. Light the charcoal</h2><p>A chimney starter is the cleanest way: fill it, light paper underneath, and pour when the top coals are ashed over. On a grill this size you rarely need a full chimney: about a half chimney does a quick cook for one or two. Skip lighter fluid; it can taste and it flares in wind. More options: <a href="/how-to-light-charcoal-without-lighter-fluid.html">lighting without lighter fluid</a>. What to burn: <a href="/best-charcoal-for-small-grills.html">charcoal vs. wood</a>.</p>
<h2>5. Build two zones</h2><p>With a 16.25" × 9" cooking area, the easiest trick is to bank most coals at one end. That gives you a hot sear zone and a cooler end to move food that's flaring or done. It's the single biggest upgrade for small-grill cooking. Full guide: <a href="/two-zone-fire-on-a-small-grill.html">two-zone fire</a>, and <a href="/charcoal-temperature-control-small-grill.html">temperature control</a>.</p>
<h2>6. Set the grates and cook</h2><p>Lay the two grill grates on top, or swap one for a <a href="{GRID}">griddle plate</a> so you can sear on one side and do eggs or onions on the other. Preheat the grates a few minutes, scrape, and wipe with an oiled paper towel held in tongs. Use an <a href="{THERMO}">instant-read thermometer</a> instead of guessing.</p>
<h2>7. Your first five cooks</h2><ol><li><a href="/smash-burgers-on-a-griddle.html">Smash burgers</a>: fast, forgiving, teaches you heat.</li><li><a href="/sausage-and-peppers-compact-grill.html">Sausage and peppers</a>: teaches the cool zone.</li><li><a href="/chicken-thighs-small-charcoal-grill.html">Chicken thighs</a>: teaches longer cooks and refueling.</li><li><a href="/reverse-sear-steak-small-grill.html">Ribeye</a>: teaches sear and slide.</li><li><a href="/campfire-breakfast-griddle.html">Griddle breakfast</a>: teaches griddle heat.</li></ol>
<h2>8. Pack it flat</h2><p>Let everything cool completely. Dump cold ash, lift off the grates, slide out the V panel, pull the end pieces, and stack. The Compact packs to about 1" thick. If you leave it set up between cooks, just pull one side panel once the ash is cold, empty it, and slide it back in. More: <a href="/how-to-clean-and-store-a-compact-grill.html">clean and store</a>.</p>
""","howto",[("How long does the Slot Grill Compact take to set up?","About 30 to 60 seconds by hand, using tab and slot assembly with no tools or hinges."),("Do I need to season the grill before first use?","It's recommended. The Compact is raw carbon steel; a seasoned coat helps food release and prevents rust."),("How much charcoal do I need for my first cook?","About a half chimney is usually enough for a quick cook for one or two people on the Compact."),("Can I use the Compact on a table?","Its 2⅞ inch air gap helps minimize radiant heat to tables, tailgates and decks, but always use a stable, non-combustible surface or fire-safe pad and follow local rules.")])
